WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.440
- What if I worked in a cafe?

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.398
How long would it take me

00:00:06.398 --> 00:00:07.473
working as a barista

00:00:07.473 --> 00:00:10.320
to earn enough money to
pay for the cappuccinos,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.020
one that we would serve in that cafe?

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.280
Well, what if we had that data,

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.000
that metric from thousands
of baristas around the world?

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.250
What if, as a bonus, we had matching data

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.810
from a little over 10 years ago?

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.600
What could it tell us about the
state of the coffee economy,

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.810
the state of our cities,
the state of all things?

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.180
We call it the Cappuccino Index.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:31.740
And here is what we learned.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:32.820
Two quick caveats.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.880
First one, the old data
set back from a decade ago

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:36.810
is a fair bit smaller.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.330
It's back from when I had a blog.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.350
People used to have blogs!

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.560
It was good back then, back
before Google Reader shut down

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:44.610
and doomed the world

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.190
to algorithmically-gated content forever.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:48.023
I digress.

00:00:48.023 --> 00:00:50.280
The second more important
caveat is around wages.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.430
We asked people for their
gross wage, not their net wage,

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:54.780
even though net wages would be

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.580
more accurate for the calculation,

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:57.707
but a lot of people just
don't know their net wage

00:00:57.707 --> 00:00:59.310
and it may vary depending on

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:02.190
how many hours they work in a week.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.800
In the US, not only did
we just ask for wage,

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.690
we asked them to exclude tips.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.060
Tips are kind of messed
up, highly variable.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:10.830
It tells you more about
the mood of your clientele

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.360
than anything else as
far as I'm concerned.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.150
So with the US specifically, bear in mind,

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.525
we just asked for wages, no tips.

00:01:18.525 --> 00:01:22.200
(coffee news jingle)

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.640
And now in the Cappuccino Index,

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.040
it's time for the headline numbers.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.080
We're going to cover
the top three countries,

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.720
the bottom three countries,
a few in between:

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.880
How many minutes do you have to work

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.260
to earn yourself a cappuccino?

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:35.640
That's the question we're asking.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.230
And we'll start with the country

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.450
where you work the least amount of time.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:41.970
We're gonna go to Australia,

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:44.790
10 minutes and five seconds only.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:48.210
That is very nearly six
cappuccinos an hour!

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:49.800
Color me impressed, Australia.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.230
Now in second place, a
little bit of a surprise.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.630
We go to Europe, to Italy!

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:57.780
Just under 12 minutes, 11:51.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:58.950
Impressed.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.410
Less of a surprise, third place,

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:02.820
back to Antipodea,

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.470
why, it's New Zealand,

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:07.020
12 minutes and 43 seconds.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:08.527
A sensible question would be,

00:02:08.527 --> 00:02:10.020
"Ah, are these just places

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.570
that pay the most to be a barista?"

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:13.800
The answer is no.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.066
But we'll talk about it,

00:02:15.066 --> 00:02:15.900
don't you worry.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.520
Let's look at the other extreme,

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.620
places you have to work the longest.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.740
And I suppose this puts it
at the bottom of the list.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.210
We only had 11 responses,

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:28.170
but on average, in India, you had to work

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:31.680
for 172 minutes and 24 seconds.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.090
That's about the duration

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.280
of Christopher Nolan's "The Odyssey."

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.200
And that is a very long time.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.360
Now in second place,
there's a bit of a drop.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.400
We come across to the Philippines,

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.713
maybe an hour less actually
at 110 minutes, 43 seconds,

00:02:44.713 --> 00:02:46.830
a pretty normal movie
runtime, I would say,

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.710
but still, a very, very, very long time.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:53.040
And third place, across the globe,

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.420
takes us to Mexico.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:56.310
74 minutes and 35 seconds.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:59.130
Another big, big drop.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:02:59.963
Maybe that's a function

00:02:59.963 --> 00:03:01.500
of the data not having enough samples.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.210
We can't really say for sure,

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:06.390
but pretty extreme as it goes.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.090
Now, that's the top three,
that's the bottom three.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:11.610
You may also wonder about other countries

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.290
that we haven't yet discussed.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:14.940
So we pulled a handful out.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.910
We thought we'd talk
about them just briefly.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:19.426
In no particular order,

00:03:19.426 --> 00:03:21.484
other than the UK's at the top,

00:03:21.484 --> 00:03:23.010
17 minutes and 30 seconds

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:25.590
for my nearest and dearest United Kingdom.

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:28.080
Quite a lot slower than
Australia, which is interesting.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.960
Quicker than the United
Kingdom is Ireland,

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.550
just under 16 minutes.

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.200
That's quite a bit faster.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:35.675
Now the United States,

00:03:35.675 --> 00:03:38.070
at 17 minutes and 55 seconds.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.080
That is gonna be messed with slightly

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:41.400
by the fact that we didn't ask people

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.860
to include tips in their earnings.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:46.440
And so that number in
reality, it may be lower.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.270
We can't say for sure.

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.910
Germany, 14 minutes and 59 seconds.

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:53.130
Pretty quick, pretty interesting.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:56.340
Now, Denmark will come up a
lot today in this whole video.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:59.820
And so we have them at 19
minutes and 24 seconds.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:03.060
Quite a long time, which
will be surprising.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.320
I promise.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:07.530
Now Canada, interesting to
compare to the United States,

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:08.363
they're neighbors,

00:04:08.363 --> 00:04:10.410
but tipping cultures may be
a little bit different there.

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:13.080
Does that explain why wages may be higher

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:14.670
and timing is shorter?

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:16.110
Oh, it's a good question.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:16.943
And then down at the bottom,

00:04:16.943 --> 00:04:19.170
we thought Singapore was
interesting to include.

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:23.130
25 minutes and 13 seconds.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.380
Now, there are obviously many countries

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.930
that we haven't put on
the screen here today.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.060
If you're curious about your
country or another country,

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:31.860
well, there's a link in the description.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:32.970
You can explore the data

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:36.570
and find out the answer to the
question that you may have.

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:37.920
And these indices are interesting

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:39.030
to compare to one another.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:41.340
The UK to Ireland, that's
an interesting comparison,

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:44.160
but a more interesting comparison

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:45.780
is to compare this data

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:48.963
to the data that we
collected 11 years ago.

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:52.020
How does it compare?

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:53.760
Couple of things about that data though.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.400
Firstly, there were way fewer
respondents to the survey.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.320
Unsurprisingly, really.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:00.720
Still enough for us to have
some good comparison points

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:02.670
around the world, which is great.

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:06.180
Second one, we did lose the raw data,

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:09.120
but, but we published a bunch of charts

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:10.290
and we labeled all the charts,

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:12.030
which was very smart of us in hindsight.

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.580
So we can, in fact, pull
the data from the charts

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.290
and use it for our comparison.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:18.840
To represent the 2015 data,

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:22.020
I'd like to introduce my younger self.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.000
So I will start with a question.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.780
Do you think the index where
you live went up or down?

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:29.040
Oh, good question.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:30.240
Thanks!

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:31.667
Put another way, do you think

00:05:31.667 --> 00:05:33.810
that a barista's wages has gone up,

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.490
which would cause the index to go down?

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:38.070
Or do you think a cappuccino's
price has gone up faster,

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.470
which would cause the index to go up?

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:42.690
Now, everyone is fully aware

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:44.250
that we have a cost of living crisis

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.140
in many, many parts of the world.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:48.390
You'll be aware that the
price of your daily coffee

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:51.420
has unquestionably gone up quite sharply.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.060
However, the results from this survey

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:55.350
were not what we expected.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:58.230
In a lot of countries, we've
seen a drop in the index.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.150
It certainly isn't universal though.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:01.200
Now, this is interesting.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.710
You can see in the UK,
the index has gone down,

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:05.760
perhaps unexpected,

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.160
but I have a theory, a little pet theory

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:09.480
behind this whole thing:

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:11.190
that of competition.

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:15.840
I think a competitive market
drives the index down.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:17.040
You look at places like London.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.650
It's not saturated here
in terms of coffee shops,

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:21.900
but it does feel pretty close.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.690
So you're arguing that
competition in a market

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:27.840
drives up wages as cafes
compete for skilled staff,

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:30.330
but stops pricing going up for coffee

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:32.670
because people are trying to
compete for customers too.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:34.170
Pretty much.

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:36.210
Now, obviously we can't ignore the fact

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:37.740
that the change is not universal.

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:40.260
You know, there are
places, unexpected places,

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.060
where the index has gone up.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:46.080
I can't look at Norway or
Denmark or Spain or Japan

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:48.270
and give you a coherent explanation

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:49.980
about why the index has increased,

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:51.120
even, as I would say,

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:54.540
competition has increased
in those markets.

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:57.240
However, I do firmly believe

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:01.320
that competition is a key
idea behind this whole thing.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:04.890
As always, your suggestions
or thoughts are very welcome

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:06.390
down in the comments below.

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:07.290
Thanks, James.

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:08.370
So you love coffee,

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:09.540
maybe the index can help you out

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:11.280
in a different sort of a way.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:15.120
If you like to travel, could
it tell you where the cheapest

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.750
or most expensive cappuccinos
in the world are to be found?

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:19.650
Well, maybe it can.

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:21.030
Here's the top three of each.

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:22.740
We're gonna be working
in reverse order today,

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:25.050
and yes, we're also going
to work in my currency.

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:26.100
I make no apologies.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:28.470
Now, Norway, in third place,
are probably pretty mad

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:32.640
about who's in second,
which is Denmark at £5.52

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:34.530
Look at this, an incredibly
expensive cappuccino,

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:37.350
and yet they are the happiest
country in the world.

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:40.290
With all of that "hygge"
and everything else.

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:41.400
Disgusting.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.590
And the top spot goes to Switzerland,

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:45.720
averaging £5.63,

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:47.880
which is a lot for a cappuccino,

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:49.620
though probably not surprising

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:51.390
if you've been to Switzerland recently.

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:52.860
To make our prestigious list,

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:55.590
we needed 10 entries
minimum from that country.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:58.590
The highest average technically
was Saudi Arabia at £6.33,

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:01.350
but we only had one entrant,
so that shouldn't count.

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:03.480
The single most expensive cappuccino

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:07.770
was from Copenhagen at £10.43!

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:09.600
But what about the cheapest?

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.430
Our third spot today on the cheapest list

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:14.400
goes to India at just £2.04

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:16.320
on average, for a cappuccino.

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:18.540
Second place on our
list is the Philippines

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:20.790
at £1.79 on average for a cappuccino,

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:21.930
which is not very much.

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:24.150
Now you might think there's a theme,

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:25.500
you can kind of guess where we're going.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:27.540
Can you guess the cheapest country?

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:29.010
I don't know if you can.

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:31.560
The top spot for the
cheapest average cappuccino

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:32.400
goes to Italy.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:34.800
Now, when I say that, you're
probably not surprised,

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:37.140
but I bet you didn't
think of it beforehand.

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:38.460
Now, a couple of important things.

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:40.710
Firstly, remember Italy being cheap.

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:42.570
That's important, it'll come up later.

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:45.660
Secondly, there's obviously a
little bit of selection bias

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:48.750
based on the kind of people
who are responding to me

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:52.667
or who know what we do
on this YouTube channel.

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:59.160
It's time now to talk about
some serious business.

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:01.950
It's time to discuss hourly earnings.

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:03.030
Who's earning the most?

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:04.860
Who's earning the least worldwide?

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:07.967
Does that drive that
country's Cappuccino Index?

00:09:07.967 --> 00:09:09.570
It's a very good question.

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:11.130
What we'll do is we'll look at each

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:12.463
as a kind of top three

00:09:12.463 --> 00:09:13.437
as a three, two, one reveal

00:09:13.437 --> 00:09:15.297
starting, I think, with the

00:09:15.297 --> 00:09:17.313
highest earners overall.

00:09:17.313 --> 00:09:19.353
And so, in third place,

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:24.960
Australia with £16.36 per hour

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:26.580
when converted to a currency

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:29.040
that's, you know,
frankly, convenient to me.

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:33.150
In second place, just
above it, is Denmark,

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:36.090
£17.07, so just a little bit more.

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:38.730
Now, you've seen the price
of a Danish cappuccino.

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:41.970
It's not cheap, but we didn't
see Australia's cappuccino

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:44.250
anywhere near the top
three most expensive.

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:45.600
Interesting stuff.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.120
Now, most relevant here,
I think most interesting,

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:50.040
Switzerland up at the top.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:53.552
Look at this! £23.48 per hour

00:09:53.552 --> 00:09:55.643
that's a substantial jump

00:09:55.643 --> 00:09:56.976
over everybody else.

00:09:56.976 --> 00:10:00.150
Now, Switzerland, clearly
an expensive place to exist.

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:02.340
And you'll remember a Swiss cappuccino,

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:03.540
also incredibly expensive.

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:06.360
But we don't really see
a perfect overlap here

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:08.460
between the highest earners

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:11.280
and their Cappuccino
Index sort of top three.

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:13.170
What about the other end of things though?

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:15.570
If we look at sort of the
lowest earning countries

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:17.100
according to the survey.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:20.130
Why, in third place, it's Mexico

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:22.800
at £2.14 per hour

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:25.860
according to the
respondents to our survey.

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:29.040
And the next one I think
will give the game away.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:31.650
Second place, the Philippines

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:35.100
with, I think, 97 pence per
hour being the earnings.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:36.870
And this is starting to
line up pretty neatly

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:39.090
with the Cappuccino Index kind
of bottom end of the table.

00:10:39.090 --> 00:10:42.098
And so we would expect in third place

00:10:42.098 --> 00:10:44.160
what we find, India.

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:48.540
India at just 71 pence
per hour on average.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:49.590
That's an enormous difference.

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:52.950
That's, essentially if
you're in Switzerland,

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:55.230
you're earning, let's say on average,

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:58.920
33 times more than a barista in India.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:01.767
And yet your cappuccino
is only something like

00:11:01.767 --> 00:11:04.110
2.8 times more expensive.

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:06.450
This is going to drive
a pretty big difference

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:08.670
in your Cappuccino Index.

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:10.290
And while this does explain the bottom end

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:11.700
of the table, I think pretty neatly,

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:14.340
this is clearly the driving
factor behind things,

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:16.530
it doesn't explain the index overall.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:19.350
Something else must be
at play, other factors.

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:21.360
We should explore some other factors.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:23.070
And to do so, I'm gonna hand you over

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:26.319
to our correspondent in the field.

00:11:26.319 --> 00:11:28.319
(birdsong)

00:11:28.319 --> 00:11:29.430
Well, what if we take this idea

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:32.460
of competition driving
down the Cappuccino Index

00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:34.770
and we look at the UK
in a bit more detail?

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:37.260
Now each cafe could be
tagged by the barista

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:40.380
who worked there as either
being urban, suburban, or rural.

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:44.190
And so we would expect the
lowest index in the urban areas.

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:45.540
And that's what we find.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:47.100
And you'd think as, you know,

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:49.740
competition decreased
as you went sort of out,

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:51.180
you'd see the index come up.

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:54.000
But that isn't what we
see because next is rural

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:55.770
and then suburban.

00:11:55.770 --> 00:11:57.900
But, probably quite importantly,

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:01.925
the differences here
are really pretty small.

00:12:01.925 --> 00:12:05.670
(faint hum of the city)

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:06.900
What about cities?

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:08.580
Well, in the UK we've got tons of data.

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:10.560
Let's start with London.

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:14.910
Now I would say London probably
has the highest density

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:17.280
of specialty coffee shops in particular.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:18.750
So it's probably the most

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:22.800
competitive coffee scene in the UK.

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:23.940
What about the other end of the scale?

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:25.800
Well, while London may have

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:27.840
the lowest index to go with that,

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:30.510
Edinburgh has the highest.

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:34.140
Is Edinburgh the least
competitive coffee city in the UK?

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:35.460
That's hard to prove or disprove.

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:37.740
I guess you'd need
information about sort of

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:41.100
number of outlets and distribution
and population densities

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:43.830
and daytime population densities.

00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:44.940
Hard to prove or disprove,

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:47.580
but if you can do either,
leave a comment down below,

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:49.320
I'd appreciate that.

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:53.070
This idea of competition
tying to the index

00:12:53.070 --> 00:12:54.330
does appeal to me.

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:56.940
And it makes me think
about the two countries,

00:12:56.940 --> 00:12:58.980
that are sort of at the top of the list

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:01.260
for having the lowest index.

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:03.033
And that's Australia and Italy.

00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:08.190
Now both of those, highly
developed, very competitive,

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:09.900
very dense coffee cultures,

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:12.540
lots and lots and lots of coffee shops.

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:13.523
But at the same time,

00:13:13.523 --> 00:13:14.581
I think they'd both argue,

00:13:14.581 --> 00:13:16.350
a real ceiling on price.

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:18.450
Now, Italy historically

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:20.760
did have a ceiling on price, I think.

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:23.640
I think there was legislation
that capped the price

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:26.790
of an espresso at the bar at one euro.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:29.010
And if you talk to Australian
coffee shop owners,

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:32.070
they definitely feel a
real cultural ceiling

00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:34.890
on what people are
willing to pay for coffee.

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:37.260
No one's really willing to push up

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:38.910
and find out that they're the one

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:42.196
who crossed that threshold first.

00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:48.420
And so what then does the
cappuccino tell us about the world

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:50.700
that we wouldn't already know?

00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:53.340
Does it add something to our
understanding of the world,

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:55.440
of coffee in particular?

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:57.120
That's a good question.

00:13:57.120 --> 00:13:59.190
Now, in this, we did think to compare it

00:13:59.190 --> 00:14:01.590
to other popular indexes.

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:04.980
We could look at GDP and
see does it correlate?

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:06.120
Yeah, pretty well.

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:08.070
There's a very popular
cost of living index.

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:09.360
Does it correlate to that?

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:10.193
Yeah, pretty well,

00:14:10.193 --> 00:14:12.540
but it doesn't really
replace either of them.

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:13.590
What does it add?

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:16.590
Well, I think it says
something about competitiveness

00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:18.750
or saturation within a coffee scene.

00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:21.630
If you look at the ones that
are kind of the top three,

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:22.800
those are most definitely

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:24.870
much more competitive coffee scenes,

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:26.460
much more saturated.

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:30.000
And I think that plays out
in the Cappuccino Index.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.880
However, you could argue
that maybe it doesn't say

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:35.760
that much about it if
you look at, say, Spain

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:37.920
or Denmark or China.

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:40.260
All of those have become
much more competitive.

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:41.790
They're all developing coffee scenes,

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:45.930
yet their index has
actually gone up, not down.

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:48.420
That said, it's hard to really know

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:50.970
where exactly within each geography

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:52.320
we're getting the data from.

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:55.230
What's the sort of quality of each cafe?

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:56.340
What's their aspiration?

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:58.440
Are they specialty? Are
they more commercial?

00:14:58.440 --> 00:14:59.700
We just don't know.

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:03.030
Coffee, as a part of our
economy, as an industry,

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:04.710
is full of these awkward questions

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:06.390
that I do think are worth asking.

00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:07.710
I don't like asking them,

00:15:07.710 --> 00:15:11.190
but I think about things
like how many kilos of coffee

00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:13.560
does a coffee producer have to grow

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:16.710
in order to make enough
money to drink just 15 grams

00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:20.853
of their own coffee served
as a filter in a London cafe?

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:23.550
That's not a question that's
gonna make you feel good,

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:24.870
but it is worth asking.

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:26.880
How many hours each day

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:28.890
does a barista have to work, typically,

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:30.393
just to pay their rent?

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:33.270
These are uncomfortable, awkward questions

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:34.380
that don't make you feel good,

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:35.790
but I think they're worth asking.

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:37.350
And I think the Cappuccino Index

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:40.260
is in the spirit of those things.

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:41.790
Back to the Cappuccino Index,

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:44.580
I do think that a little
bit more frequency

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:45.870
in asking the question

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:48.660
would improve the value of the answer.

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:51.690
We've waited 11 years
and we can see change.

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:54.900
But if we asked this each
year, then I think we'd reveal

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:57.480
much more, sort of interesting insights

00:15:57.480 --> 00:15:59.490
in the way that the world is changing.

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:02.010
And that may be where it is most useful.

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:03.210
And if you think that's
something you'd like to see,

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:05.010
then leave me a comment down below.

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:06.300
Should we try and do this every year

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:08.400
or at least every couple of years?

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:10.620
Are there other questions
that we didn't answer

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:14.700
that could be answered by the
data in the Cappuccino Index?

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:17.220
Linked in the description is the data set.

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:18.870
If you want to go and explore and ask

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:20.400
and answer some of your own questions too.

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:22.140
If you find something interesting,

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:25.710
do please leave us a comment down below.

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:29.460
But for now, I'll say thank
you so much for watching,

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:31.060
and I hope you have a great day.

